::CW_U03_L01_Q01::Passage: Nora returned the trophy after learning the scorekeeper had made a mistake. Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Trophies are always heavy.#Incorrect. The passage is about honesty, not weight.
=B. Doing what is right can matter more than receiving praise.#Correct. Nora gives up the trophy because honesty matters.
~C. Scorekeepers never make errors.#Incorrect. The passage says an error happened.
~D. Competitions have no themes.#Incorrect. A competition can develop a theme through choice.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q02::Passage: Jalen hid his poem for weeks. When his grandmother asked to hear it, he read it with shaking hands. What theme is strongest?{
~A. Poems should always be hidden.#Incorrect. The action moves from hiding to sharing.
~B. Grandmothers always write poems.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
~C. Hands are the main topic.#Incorrect. Shaking hands show fear, not the theme alone.
=D. Courage can begin before fear disappears.#Correct. Jalen is afraid but still reads the poem.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q03::Passage: Sari saved the last bus seat for a stranger carrying two bags, though her own feet hurt. What topic and theme fit best?{
=A. Topic: kindness; Theme: Small sacrifices can show real compassion.#Correct. Sari gives up comfort for someone else.
~B. Topic: buses; Theme: Buses have seats.#Incorrect. That is factual, not thematic.
~C. Topic: strangers; Theme: Never speak to anyone.#Incorrect. The passage shows helpfulness.
~D. Topic: bags; Theme: Bags are difficult.#Incorrect. The bags are context, not the universal idea.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q04::Passage: After losing the election, Priya stayed to help hang the winner's posters. Which theme statement is strongest?{
~A. Elections use posters.#Incorrect. This names a detail, not a theme.
~B. Posters are more important than people.#Incorrect. The focus is Priya's response.
=C. Losing with grace can reveal character.#Correct. Priya's action after losing shows maturity.
~D. Priya should never run again.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q05::Passage: Mateo wanted to throw away the broken guitar, but he repaired one string each night until it played again. What theme is best supported?{
=A. Patience can turn damage into possibility.#Correct. Mateo repairs the guitar over time.
~B. Broken guitars are useless forever.#Incorrect. He repairs it.
~C. Music has no connection to theme.#Incorrect. The guitar helps develop the theme.
~D. Night is always dangerous.#Incorrect. Night is not the central idea.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q06::Passage: Kendra crossed out the lie in her letter and wrote the truth underneath. Which evidence best supports a theme about honesty?{
~A. The letter exists.#Incorrect. A letter alone does not prove honesty.
~B. The sentence uses the word underneath.#Incorrect. Word position is not the main evidence.
~C. Kendra owns a pencil.#Incorrect. No pencil is mentioned.
=D. Kendra replaces the lie with the truth.#Correct. Her revision shows an honest choice.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q07::Passage: The village shared one lantern during the blackout. Each family used it for one hour, then carried it to the next door. What theme is strongest?{
~A. Lanterns are expensive.#Incorrect. Cost is not discussed.
=B. Communities survive hardship by sharing limited resources.#Correct. The families take turns helping one another.
~C. Darkness means nothing.#Incorrect. The blackout creates a need.
~D. Doors are the theme.#Incorrect. Doors are setting details.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q08::Passage: Alina kept practicing the difficult dance after the mirror cracked and the music stopped. Which topic is most central?{
~A. Mirrors only.#Incorrect. The mirror is an obstacle detail.
~B. Weather.#Incorrect. Weather is not mentioned.
=C. Persistence.#Correct. Alina continues despite obstacles.
~D. Cooking.#Incorrect. Cooking is not mentioned.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q09::Passage: When the class laughed at his wrong answer, Ben asked the next question anyway. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Wrong answers are always funny.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on Ben's response.
~B. Questions should never be asked.#Incorrect. Ben asks another question.
~C. Classrooms have no conflicts.#Incorrect. The laughter creates conflict.
=D. Learning requires the courage to continue after embarrassment.#Correct. Ben keeps participating after being laughed at.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q10::Passage: Imani found the old recipe card after the bakery closed. She baked the bread at home so the smell would not disappear. What theme is strongest?{
=A. Traditions can continue when people choose to carry them forward.#Correct. Imani keeps the bakery memory alive through action.
~B. Bakeries never close.#Incorrect. This one did close.
~C. Recipe cards are always new.#Incorrect. The card is old.
~D. Smells cannot matter in stories.#Incorrect. Sensory details can develop theme.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q11::Passage: Eli forgave his friend but still asked for time before things felt normal again. Which theme statement is most precise?{
~A. Friends should never disagree.#Incorrect. The passage shows conflict and repair.
~B. Time is a clock.#Incorrect. This is not the passage meaning.
=C. Forgiveness can begin before trust is fully rebuilt.#Correct. Eli forgives but still needs time.
~D. Forgiveness is impossible.#Incorrect. Eli forgives.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q12::Passage: Maya carried water to the smallest plants first because she knew the older trees had deeper roots. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Trees never need water.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
=B. The strongest sometimes need less help than the vulnerable.#Correct. Maya helps the plants that need support most.
~C. Buckets are the main topic.#Incorrect. No bucket is named.
~D. Plants cannot develop themes.#Incorrect. Plant care can symbolize human ideas.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q13::Passage: Aiden kept the team secret until it hurt someone else. Then he told the coach everything. What theme is strongest?{
=A. Loyalty becomes harmful when it protects wrongdoing.#Correct. Aiden changes when secrecy hurts someone.
~B. Teams never have secrets.#Incorrect. This team did.
~C. Coaches know everything already.#Incorrect. Aiden tells the coach.
~D. Secrets are always harmless.#Incorrect. The secret hurts someone.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q14::Passage: The narrator says, "I wanted the room to remember me, so I carved my initials into the desk. The next day, I sanded them smooth." What theme is best supported?{
~A. Desks are impossible to repair.#Incorrect. The narrator sands the initials smooth.
~B. Initials are the only theme.#Incorrect. Initials are a detail.
~C. Rooms remember everything literally.#Incorrect. The line is figurative.
=D. People can learn that leaving a mark should not mean causing damage.#Correct. The narrator repairs the harm after wanting attention.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q15::Passage: Hana gave her speech in her home language first, then translated it herself. Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Translation means hiding identity.#Incorrect. She honors identity through both choices.
~B. Speeches should have no language.#Incorrect. Speech requires language.
=C. Identity can be honored while still inviting others to understand.#Correct. Hana keeps her language and translates for others.
~D. Home languages are unimportant.#Incorrect. The passage shows importance.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q16::Passage: Nico wanted revenge, but he returned the borrowed jacket without tearing it. Which theme is strongest?{
~A. Jackets cause revenge.#Incorrect. The jacket is part of the conflict, not the cause.
=B. Self-control can stop anger from becoming harm.#Correct. Nico feels anger but chooses not to damage the jacket.
~C. Borrowing is always wrong.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that.
~D. Nico has no feelings.#Incorrect. He wants revenge.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q17::Passage: The new mayor painted over her own campaign slogan so the town mural could include everyone's handprints. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Murals should have no paint.#Incorrect. The passage includes painting.
~B. Campaigns never end.#Incorrect. She paints over the slogan.
~C. Handprints are always political.#Incorrect. Here they show community.
=D. Leadership can mean making room for more voices than one's own.#Correct. The mayor removes self-promotion for community participation.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q18::Passage: After the storm, Omar rebuilt his neighbor's fence before fixing his own mailbox. What theme is strongest?{
=A. Helping others after hardship can show shared responsibility.#Correct. Omar prioritizes a neighbor's need after damage.
~B. Mailboxes are more important than fences.#Incorrect. Omar chooses the fence first.
~C. Storms fix fences.#Incorrect. The storm damages things.
~D. Neighbors never help each other.#Incorrect. Omar helps.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q19::Passage: Lila stepped off the winning platform to help the runner who had fallen at the finish line. Which theme fits best?{
~A. Winning platforms are dangerous.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that.
=B. Compassion can matter more than public victory.#Correct. Lila leaves the platform to help someone hurt.
~C. Falling means a person loses all value.#Incorrect. Lila's action shows the opposite.
~D. Races cannot have themes.#Incorrect. Actions in races can develop themes.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q20::Passage: The old clock stopped the day Grandpa left. Years later, Mina wound it and listened until it began again. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Clocks never stop.#Incorrect. This clock stopped.
~B. Time is not part of stories.#Incorrect. The clock helps develop meaning.
=C. Healing can involve returning to memories that once felt painful.#Correct. Mina restarts the clock connected to loss.
~D. Grandpa fixes the clock.#Incorrect. Mina winds it.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q21::Passage: Tori admitted she was lost instead of pretending she knew the trail. Which theme is strongest?{
=A. Asking for help can be wiser than protecting pride.#Correct. Tori chooses honesty over pretending.
~B. Trails are always easy.#Incorrect. She is lost.
~C. Pride solves every problem.#Incorrect. Pride would keep her pretending.
~D. Maps are required in every theme.#Incorrect. No map is mentioned.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q22::Passage: The town planted flowers where the burned library had stood. What theme does this image most likely develop?{
~A. Libraries are gardens.#Incorrect. The image is symbolic, not literal.
~B. Fire is always good.#Incorrect. The burned library suggests loss.
=C. Renewal can grow from loss.#Correct. Flowers replace a damaged place with new life.
~D. Flowers erase history completely.#Incorrect. They respond to history, not erase it.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q23::Passage: Which answer is a complete theme statement?{
~A. Friendship.#Incorrect. This is a topic, not a statement.
~B. The story is about a friend.#Incorrect. This is summary.
~C. The friend wore blue.#Incorrect. This is a detail.
=D. Friendship can require honesty even when honesty is uncomfortable.#Correct. This is a complete, arguable idea about friendship.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q24::Passage: "Ravi wanted the shortcut, but he waited for his younger cousin to tie her shoe." Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Shoes are the only topic.#Incorrect. The shoe creates the situation, not the full theme.
=B. Patience can show care for another person's pace.#Correct. Ravi gives up speed to wait for his cousin.
~C. Shortcuts always fail.#Incorrect. The shortcut is not judged.
~D. Cousins cannot walk together.#Incorrect. The passage shows them together.
}
::CW_U03_L01_Q25::Passage: A student writes, "The theme is that Kai finds a coin." What feedback is best?{
=A. Revise because that is a plot detail; explain what the coin suggests about a larger human idea.#Correct. A theme needs a broader message supported by the detail.
~B. Keep it because all plot details are themes.#Incorrect. Plot details can support themes but are not themes by themselves.
~C. Remove all evidence from the answer.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
~D. Change the answer to one word only.#Incorrect. One-word topics are not complete themes.
}
